delta-change "typed-captures"
message "add TypedCaptures cardinality-aware captures and typed constructor fields"
author "ai"
doc "guide" """
  |# TypedCaptures: cardinality-aware captures
  |
  |TypedCaptures is the third post-constitution grammar product. It is derived from the
  |validated GrammarCombinators product graph and remains outside the frozen F0-F11 bootstrap
  |constitution.
  |
  |## Frozen product identity
  |
  |- GrammarCombinators predecessor root: `c000ed72cf1fe773add80a64adb2aa655b0ce0b528d3c4113b01e6c041825d05`
  |- TypedCaptures delta: `5325be5f01926b2a0520b7b9447f0baa137cfefe62db01c7a944e9ff8daae4f2`
  |- TypedCaptures root: `c636319db67af7225fcdc617e2346fc60698992e9766393ae75441241dd55728`
  |- Graph size: 386 nodes, 455 edges, 386 entities
  |
  |TypedCaptures is derived only as:
  |
  |```text
  |GrammarCombinators + TypedCaptures.delta = TypedCaptures
  |```
  |
  |It does not alter any F0-F11 foundation identity or any earlier product root.
  |
  |## Why TypedCaptures exists
  |
  |GrammarCombinators introduced graph-resident `optional` and `repeat` combinators, but they
  |were deliberately capture-free. They could consume surface syntax, yet they
  |could not say that an AST field was optional or repeated without the host
  |inventing an implicit collection convention.
  |
  |TypedCaptures removes that hidden convention. Cardinality is semantic graph data.
  |
  |```text
  |reference        -> T
  |capture(one)     -> T
  |capture(optional)-> Option[T]
  |capture(many)    -> Vector[T]
  |```
  |
  |The Scala host contains a generic field-value representation, but the choice of
  |cardinality for a concrete constructor field comes from the Trellis graph.
  |
  |## First-class constructor fields
  |
  |Constructor fields are no longer encoded by a string attribute such as:
  |
  |```text
  |fields = left:Expr;right:Expr
  |```
  |
  |TypedCaptures introduces `language.constructor-field` nodes. Each field is connected
  |by typed edges to:
  |
  |1. its owning constructor,
  |2. its language sort, and
  |3. its cardinality.
  |
  |The graph therefore describes a field such as `All.items` as:
  |
  |```text
  |All --constructor-field--> items
  |items --field-sort-------> Expr
  |items --field-cardinality> many
  |```
  |
  |Existing Bool0 scalar fields are migrated to the same representation with
  |cardinality `one`.
  |
  |## Cardinalities
  |
  |TypedCaptures provides three graph-resident values for Bool0:
  |
  |- `one`
  |- `optional`
  |- `many`
  |
  |A `grammar.capture` node binds a grammar expression to an AST field and carries
  |`min` and `max` bounds. The bounds must agree with the cardinality:
  |
  |- `one`: exactly `1..1`
  |- `optional`: exactly `0..1`
  |- `many`: any valid `min..max` interval
  |
  |Invalid combinations are rejected fail-closed by the generic language
  |validator.
  |
  |## Bool0 examples
  |
  |TypedCaptures adds two constructors only to exercise cardinality semantics.
  |
  |### Optional capture
  |
  |```text
  |maybe [ ]
  |maybe [ true ]
  |```
  |
  |These produce, respectively:
  |
  |```text
  |Maybe(value = None)
  |Maybe(value = Some(True))
  |```
  |
  |The brackets are intentional. Without an explicit boundary, a surface such as
  |`all [ maybe true ]` could be interpreted either as one `Maybe(Some(True))`
  |item or as two repeated items, `Maybe(None)` and `True`. TypedCaptures resolves that
  |ambiguity in the grammar itself rather than adding an invisible parser
  |preference.
  |
  |### Repeated capture
  |
  |```text
  |all [ true ! false maybe [ true ] ]
  |```
  |
  |produces an `All` tree whose `items` field is a vector containing three Expr
  |values: `True`, `Not(False)`, and `Maybe(Some(True))`.
  |
  |The current Bool0 example bounds `items` to at most eight values so malformed or
  |runaway grammar edits remain easy to test. The cardinality model itself is not
  |specific to that bound.
  |
  |## Printing
  |
  |Printing consumes the same cardinality declarations used by parsing.
  |
  |- `one` prints one captured tree,
  |- `optional` prints zero or one captured tree according to the AST value,
  |- `many` prints every captured tree in order.
  |
  |No constructor-specific `Maybe` or `All` printer case exists in Scala.
  |
  |## Compatibility
  |
  |GrammarCombinators remains independently materialized as `Language.grammar-combinators` and is used
  |as a differential oracle. Existing TemplateGrammar, ExpressionGrammar, and GrammarCombinators trees retain
  |their scalar `fields` view, while TypedCaptures exposes cardinal values through
  |`Tree.fieldValue` / `Tree.fieldValues`.
  |
  |This preserves the previous product tests while allowing new AST shapes.
  |
  |## Free changes
  |
  |`DeltaBool0` is extended to admit semantic edits of:
  |
  |- `language.constructor-field`
  |- `grammar.capture`
  |- `grammar.cardinality`
  |
  |The normal language boundary and full post-change validation still apply. For
  |example, changing an optional capture to bounds `0..2` is rejected because the
  |graph would no longer agree with the field's `optional` cardinality.
  |
  |## Acceptance
  |
  |The TypedCaptures slice is accepted when:
  |
  |1. the Scala suite reports 164 tests and zero failures;
  |2. `TypedCaptures.delta` has its frozen canonical change ID;
  |3. replay over GrammarCombinators yields the frozen TypedCaptures root;
  |4. optional and many captures parse and print round-trip;
  |5. GrammarCombinators and TypedCaptures remain observationally equal on the GrammarCombinators surface;
  |6. invalid cardinality edits fail closed; and
  |7. the existing Scala/Rust F0-F11 bootstrap parity harness remains byte-identical.
  |
  """
